@node Naming system
@section Naming system

In ZIL, variable names use @code{case-insensitive} naming (conventionally @code{lower-case-with-hyphens}).
All keywords are case-insensitive but conventionally written in uppercase.

@table @asis
@item Keywords: <IF>, <ELSE>, <THEN>, <WHILE>, <FOR>, <BREAK>, <CONTINUE>, <RETURN>, <DEFINE>, <SET>, <SETG>, <GET>, <PUT>, <CALL>, <GO>, <EXIT>, <LABEL>, < ASSERT>, <WAIT>, <STOP>, <START>, <FADE>, <RESTORE>, <SAVE>, <RESTART>, <VERSION>, <OBJECT>, <PROPERTY>, <GLOBAL>, <LOCAL>
@item Operators: +, -, *, /, =, <>, <, >, <=, >=, AND, OR, NOT
@item String delimiters: Double quotes (@code{"..."}) for strings, single quotes (@code{'...'}) for atoms or characters
@item Comments: Semicolon (;) to end of line
@end table

@node Basic structure
@section Basic structure for a stand-alone routine or program

A ZIL program consists of routines (functions) and object definitions, and is executed by the Z-machine interpreter.

@example
; Initialize game
<SETG SCORE 0>
<SETG LIVES-LEFT 3>
<SETG CURRENT-ROOM 1>

<ROUTINE INITIALIZE-GAME>
    <SETG SCORE 0>
    <SETG LIVES-LEFT 3>
    <SETG CURRENT-ROOM 1>
</ROUTINE>

<ROUTINE PROCESS-INPUT>
    ; Simulate input handling
    <IF> <AND> <GTEQ> <INPUT> "move" <EQUAL> <INPUT> "north">
        <SETG CURRENT-ROOM> <ADD> <GSET CURRENT-ROOM> 10
    </IF>
</ROUTINE>

<ROUTINE UPDATE-GAME>
    ; Game logic updates
</ROUTINE>

<ROUTINE RENDER-DISPLAY>
    ; Rendering code
</ROUTINE>

<ROUTINE MAIN>
    <INITIALIZE-GAME>
    <WHILE> <NOT> <GAME-OVER>
        <PROCESS-INPUT>
        <UPDATE-GAME>
        <RENDER-DISPLAY>
    </WHILE>
</ROUTINE>
@end example

@node Basic structure object
@section Basic structure for an object method

ZIL supports object-oriented programming through objects and properties, with routines that operate on objects.

@example
; Define an object (e.g., a player)
<OBJECT PLAYER
    <PROPERTY X 0>
    <PROPERTY Y 0>
    <PROPERTY SPRITE 0>
    <DEFAULT>
>

; Define a routine to move the player
<ROUTINE MOVE-PLAYER <OBJ> <DX> <DY>>
    <SET> <P> <OBJ> <X>> <ADD> <GET> <P> <OBJ> <X>> <DX>
    <SET> <P> <OBJ> <Y>> <ADD> <GET> <P> <OBJ> <Y>> <DY>
</ROUTINE>

; Create instance (implicitly by defining the object)
; Access via the object name
</example>

@node Calling routines
@section Calling a routine (nullary)

To call a routine that takes no arguments:

@example
<INITIALIZE-GRAPHICS>
<CLEAR-SCREEN>
<PLAY-STARTUP-MUSIC>
<SOME-ROUTINE>
@end example

@node Calling routines value
@section Calling a routine with a value (in accumulator)

To call a routine that returns a single value (in accumulator):

@example
<SETG SCORE> <CALCULATE-BONUS> <GSET LEVEL> <GSET TIME-LEFT>
<SETG BONUS-FACTOR> <GET-INPUT-FACTOR>
<SETG VOLUME> <GET-MASTER-VOLUME>
<SOME-FUNCTION>
@end example

@node Calling routines giving
@section Calling a routine giving a value (in accumulator)

Not applicable in ZIL's standard calling convention.

@node Calling routines values
@section Calling a routine with values in and out (in accumulator)

To call a routine with parameters:

@example
<DRAW-SPRITE> <GSET SPRITE-ID> <GSET X-POS> <GSET Y-POS>
<PLAY-SOUND> <GSET SOUND-ID> <GSET VOLUME> <GSET LOOP-FLAG>
<CHECK-COLLISION> <GSET OBJ1> <GSET OBJ2> <GSET RESULT-VAR>
<SOME-ROUTINE> <PARAM1> <PARAM2>
@end example

@node Calling library
@section Calling a library routine (all 4 styles)

ZIL accesses library routines through standard routine calls or object property manipulation:

@example
<LOAD-LEVEL> <GSET LEVEL-NUMBER>          @r{Direct call}
<FAR-PROC> <GSET LIBRARY-ADDRESS>         @r{Far call>
<SETG RESULT> <LIBRARY-FUNCTION> <GSET INPUT>  @r{Library function>
; Object method call: manipulate object properties directly or via routines
</example>

These correspond to:
@table @asis
@item Direct: RoutineName(...)
@item Far: AbsoluteAddress(...) (via external interface)
@item Library: LibraryEntry(...) or FunctionName(...)
@item Object: Direct property manipulation or specialized routines
@end table

@node Calling far bank
@section Calling a far bank Service (all 4 styles)

Far routines in ZIL are called using absolute addresses or through
the Z-machine's call/vcall mechanism for far routines:

@example
; Assume a far routine at address 0x8000
<FAR> <CALL> <ADDRESS> #x8000 <ARGUMENTS>
@end example

These correspond to:
@table @asis
@item Direct: Not used for far calls
@item Far: FAR CALL to absolute address
@item Library: Not applicable
@item Object: Not applicable
@end table

@node Invoking object
@section Invoking a method on an object (all 4 styles)

Object method invocation in ZIL is done by manipulating object properties
or calling routines that take the object as an argument:

@example
<SET> <P> <PLAYER> <X>> 10
<SET> <P> <PLAYER> <Y>> 10
<MOVE-PLAYER> <PLAYER> 5 10
<SET> <GAME-OVER> <IS-GAME-OVER> <GAME-STATE>
</example>

These correspond to:
@table @asis
@item Direct: Setting object properties via <SET> and <P>
@item Far: Not applicable for methods (objects typically in same memory)
@item Library: Method may call library routines internally
@item Object: Routine invocation with object as argument
@end table

@node Assignment
@section Assigning to a variable

Variables are assigned using <SET> for local variables or <SETG> for global variables:

@example
<SETG SCORE 0>                     @r{Explicit global assignment}
<SETG LIVES-LEFT 3>                @r{Implicit global assignment}
<SET COUNTER> <ADD> <GET COUNTER> 1> @r{Increment local}
<SET LIVES-LEFT> <SUB> <GET LIVES-LEFT> 1> @r{Decrement local}
<SETG SCORE> <ADD> <GSET SCORE> 10> @r{Arithmetic assignment global}
<SETG MESSAGE> "Hello, World!"     @r{String assignment global}
@end example

@node Assignment object
@section Assigning to an object slot

Object slots (properties) are assigned using <SET> with <P> (property) and the object:

@example
<SET> <P> <PLAYER> <X>> 160        @r{Set object's x coordinate}
<SET> <P> <PLAYER> <Y>> 100        @r{Set object's y coordinate}
<SET> <P> <PLAYER> <SPRITE>> 5     @r{Set sprite number}
<SET> <P> <PLAYER> <VISIBLE>> #t   @r{Set visibility flag}
</example>

@node Assignment array
@section Assigning to an array member with a subscript

ZIL uses tables (arrays) and lists. Elements are assigned using <SET> with <T> (table) or <L> (list):

@example
<SET> <T> <SCORE-LIST> (MAKE-TABLE 10 0)>
<SET> <T> <INITIALS-LIST> (MAKE-TABLE 10 "">
<SET> <T> <BOARD> (MAKE-TABLE 8 8 0)>

<SET> <T> <SCORE-LIST> 0> 100
<SET> <T> <INITIALS-LIST> 0> "ALI"
<SET> <T> <BOARD> 2 3> 1
</example>

@node Arithmetic
@section Basic arithmetic: addition and subtraction

Basic arithmetic uses the + and - operators (via <ADD> and <SUB>):

@example
<SETG SCORE> <ADD> <GSET SCORE> 10>                  @r{Addition}
<SETG LIVES-LEFT> <SUB> <GSET LIVES-LEFT> 1>         @r{Subtraction}
<SETG TOTAL> <ADD> <GSET WIDTH> <GSET HEIGHT>        @r{Add two variables}
<SETG FINAL-PRICE> <SUB> <GSET PRICE> <GSET DISCOUNT> @r{Subtraction}
<SETG DELTA> <SUB> <GSET NEW-VALUE> <GSET OLD-VALUE> @r{Subtraction>
@end example

@node Arithmetic bit
@section Basic arithmetic: bit-shifting for mutiplication or division

Bit-shifting for multiplication/division by powers of two is not directly supported.
Use multiplication/division or external routines:

@example
<SETG RESULT> <MULT> <GSET VALUE> 4>                 @r{Left shift 2 bits (multiply by 4)}
<SETG RESULT> <DIV> <GSET VALUE> 2>                  @r{Right shift 1 bit (divide by 2)}
<SETG RESULT> <MULT> <GSET VALUE> 8>                 @r{Multiply by 8}
<SETG RESULT> <DIV> <GSET VALUE> 16>                 @r{Divide by 16}
</example>

@node Complex arithmetic
@section Complex arithmetic: multiple expressions

Complex arithmetic expressions follow standard precedence rules:

@example
<SETG TOTAL> <ADD> <MULT> <GSET BASE-SALARY> 12> <ADD> <GSET BONUS> <SUB> <GSET DEDUCTIONS>>
<SETG AVERAGE> <DIV> <ADD> <ADD> <GSET SUM1> <GSET SUM2> <GSET SUM3> 3.0>
<SETG DISTANCE> <SQRT> <ADD> <SQUARE> <SUB> <GSET X2> <GSET X1> <SQUARE> <SUB> <GSET Y2> <GSET Y1>>
</example>

@node Boolean
@section Boolean expressions: equality and inequalities

Boolean expressions use logical operators and comparison operators:

@example
<IF> <G> <GSET SCORE> 1000> ... </IF>                  @r{Greater than}
<IF> <L> <GSET SCORE> 50> ... </IF>                    @r{Less than}
<IF> <E> <GSET SCORE> 100> ... </IF>                   @r{Equality}
<IF> <NE> <GSET SCORE> 0> ... </IF>                    @r{Inequality}
<IF> <AND> <GE> <GSET X> 0> <LT> <GSET X> <GSET MAX-X> ... </IF> @r{Combined conditions}
<IF> <OR> <E> <GSET BUTTON> <GSET BUTTON-A> <E> <GSET BUTTON> <GSET BUTTON-B> ... </IF> @r{Alternative conditions}
</example>

@node If then else
@section Using if/then/else structures and examine/cond structures

Conditional execution uses <IF>/<THEN>/<ELSE>/<IF> blocks:

@example
<IF> <G> <GSET TEMPERATURE> <GSET THOT>
    <THEN>
        <SETG STATUS> "HOT"
    <ELSE>
        <IF> <G> <GSET TEMPERATURE> <GSET TCOLD>
            <THEN>
                <SETG STATUS> "WARM"
            <ELSE>
                <SETG STATUS> "COLD"
            </IF>
        </IF>
    </IF>
</example>

; ZIL does not have a built-in CASE statement, but we can use nested IF/THEN/ELSE/IF
<IF> <OR> <E> <GSET ANSWER> "Y" <E> <GSET ANSWER> "y">
    <THEN>
        <SETG RESPONSE> "YES"
    <ELSE>
        <IF> <OR> <E> <GSET ANSWER> "N" <E> <GSET ANSWER> "n">
            <THEN>
                <SETG RESPONSE> "NO"
            <ELSE>
                <SETG RESPONSE> "INVALID"
            </IF>
        </IF>
    </IF>
</example>

@node Performing
@section Performing loops

Loops are implemented using <WHILE> and <FOR> loops:

@example
; <WHILE> loop equivalent (pre-test)
<SET> <I> 1>
<WHILE> <L> <GET I> 10>
    <PRINT> "Count: " <GET I>
    <SET> <I> <ADD> <GET I> 1>
</WHILE>

; <FOR> loop equivalent (known iterations)
<FOR> <I> 1 10
    <PRINT> "Count: " <GET I>
</FOR>

; Loop with BREAK and CONTINUE
<SET> <I> 1
<WHILE> <L> <GET I> 100
    <IF> <E> <GET> <T> <SCORE-LIST> <GET I>> <GSET TARGET-VALUE>
        <THEN>
            <PRINT> "Found at index: " <GET I>
            <BREAK>   @r{Exit loop immediately}
        </IF>
    <IF> <L> <GET> <T> <SCORE-LIST> <GET I>> 0
        <THEN>
            <CONTINUE>   @r{Skip to next iteration}
        </IF>
    ; Process positive values
    <SET> <I> <ADD> <GET I> 1
</WHILE>
</example>

@node Breaking loops
@section Breaking out of loops safely

Early loop exit is achieved using <BREAK> and <CONTINUE> statements:

@example
Exit <WHILE> loop early
<SET> <I> 1
<WHILE> <L> <GET I> 100
    <IF> <E> <GET> <T> <SCORE-LIST> <GET I>> <GSET TARGET-VALUE>
        <THEN>
            <PRINT> "Found at index: " <GET I>
            <BREAK>   @r{Exit loop immediately}
        </IF>
    <SET> <I> <ADD> <GET I> 1
</WHILE>

; Skip current iteration
<SET> <I> 1
<WHILE> <L> <GET I> 100
    <IF> <L> <GET> <T> <SCORE-LIST> <GET I>> 0
        <THEN>
            <CONTINUE>   @r{Skip to next iteration}
        </IF>
    <SET> <I> <ADD> <GET I> 1
</WHILE>

; Nested loop control
<SET> <I> 1
<WHILE> <L> <GET I> 10
    <SET> <J> 1
    <WHILE> <L> <GET J> 10
        <IF> <E> <GET> <T> <GET> <T> <MATRIX> <GET I>> <GET J>> 0
            <THEN>
                <BREAK>   @r{Exit inner loop only}
            </IF>
        <SET> <J> <ADD> <GET J> 1
    </WHILE>
    <SET> <I> <ADD> <GET I> 1
</WHILE>
</example>

@node Dialogue
@section Calling a dialogue script (Forth scripts)

ZIL does not directly call Forth dialogue scripts. Instead, it uses
its own routines. However, through the semantic-sugar
layer, ZIL commands can be mapped to calls of the shared Fountain/Forth
runtime library:

@example
; Original ZIL code
<IF> <AND> <G> <GSET X> 100 <L> <GSET X> 200>
    <THEN>
        <SHOW-MESSAGE> <GSET MSG-WELCOME>
    </IF>
</example>

; Desugared via semantic-sugar layer
<IF> <AND> <G> <GSET X> 100 <L> <GSET X> 200>
    <THEN>
        <:call-acc :target "ShowWelcomeMessage" :using nil>
    </IF>
</example>

@node Tiles
@section Manipulating tiles

Tile manipulation is performed through direct video memory access
or through library routines for tiled backgrounds:

@example
; Direct memory access (mode-dependent)
; Assuming direct memory access via pointer
<SET> <VIDEO-MEMORY> #xB8000
<SET> <OFFSET> <MULT> <ADD> <MULT> <GSET ROW> 80> <GSET COL> 2>
<SET> <TILE-NUM> 42>
<SET> <ATTRIBUTE> 7>
<SET> <BYTE> <VIDEO-MEMORY> <OFFSET>> <TILE-NUM>
<SET> <BYTE> <VIDEO-MEMORY> <ADD> <OFFSET> 1> <ATTRIBUTE>

; Library routine approach
<LOAD-TILE-SET> <GSET TILESET-ID>
<PLACE-TILE> <GSET LAYER> <GSET X> <GSET Y> <GSET TILE-INDEX>
<SCROLL-BACKGROUND> <GSET DX> <GSET DY>
</example>

@node Decals
@section Manipulating decals

Decals (sprites or movable objects) are manipulated using:

@example
; Hardware sprite approach (if available)
<INIT-SPRITES>
<SET-SPRITE-POSITION> <GSET SPRITE-ID> <GSET X> <GSET Y>
<SET-SPRITE-IMAGE> <GSET SPRITE-ID> <GSET IMAGE-ID>
<SHOW-SPRITE> <GSET SPRITE-ID> -1   @r{-1 = visible}

; Software sprite approach
; Define a sprite structure (as a table or object)
<SET> <SPRITE> (MAKE-OBJECT)
<SET> <P> <SPRITE> <X>> 100
<SET> <P> <SPRITE> <Y>> 50
<SET> <P> <SPRITE> <FRAME>> 0

; Update
<SET> <P> <SPRITE> <X>> 100
<SET> <P> <SPRITE> <Y>> 50
<SET> <P> <SPRITE> <FRAME>> <MOD> <ADD> <GET> <P> <SPRITE> <FRAME>> 1> 4
</example>

@node Objects
@section Manipulating objects

Object manipulation depends on the implementation approach:

@example
; Define a game object class (as a template object)
<OBJECT GAME-OBJECT
    <PROPERTY X 0>
    <PROPERTY Y 0>
    <PROPERTY WIDTH 0>
    <PROPERTY HEIGHT 0>
    <PROPERTY ACTIVE #t>
    <DEFAULT>
>

; Create instances
<SET> <GAME-OBJECTS> (MAKE-LIST 20)
<FOR> <I> 0 19
    <SET> <OBJ> (MAKE-OBJECT <GAME-OBJECT>)
    <SET> <P> <OBJ> <X>> 100
    <SET> <P> <OBJ> <Y>> 100
    <SET> <P> <OBJ> <WIDTH>> 16
    <SET> <P> <OBJ> <HEIGHT>> 16
    <SET> <T> <GAME-OBJECTS> <I>> <OBJ>
</FOR>

; Update object position
<FOR> <I> 0 19
    <SET> <OBJ> <T> <GAME-OBJECTS> <I>>
    <SET> <P> <OBJ> <X>> <ADD> <GET> <P> <OBJ> <X>> <GSET DX>
    <SET> <P> <OBJ> <Y>> <ADD> <GET> <P> <OBJ> <Y>> <GSET DY>
</FOR>

; Check active status and draw
<FOR> <I> 0 19
    <SET> <OBJ> <T> <GAME-OBJECTS> <I>>
    <IF> <GET> <P> <OBJ> <ACTIVE>>
        <THEN>
            <DRAW-OBJECT> <OBJ>
        </IF>
</FOR>
</example>

@node Unsupported
@section Unsupported language features

The following ZIL features are not supported in this EIGHTBOL release:

@table @asis
@item File I/O operations (OPEN, READ, WRITE, CLOSE)
@item Graphics commands (DRAW-LINE, DRAW-CIRCLE, FILL-POLYGON, BLIT)
@item Hardware-specific features (JOYSTICK, MOUSE, KEYBOARD beyond basic)
@item Extended memory handling (unless emulated)
@item Multithreading or concurrent execution
@item Exception handling (TRY, CATCH, FINALLY, THROW)
@item Pointer arithmetic and manual memory management
@item Preprocessor directives (#DEFINE, #IFDEF)
@item Object-oriented multiple inheritance (unless using external library)
@item Sound and music commands (unless using external library)
@end table

